  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Addressing marine hypoxia in the Bay of Bengal Large Marine Ecosystem by reducing land-based agricultural pollution in the north-west of Sri Lanka</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">265. The project is anticipated to benefit shrimp farm production and farmers practicing rice cultivation in the Kala and Mee Oya river basins.  This will be achieved through promoting best management practices, strengthening policy environment, increasing financial opportunities including private sector participation, and generating increased awareness and capacity building activities in relation to land-based agricultural pollution.  This will reduce the risk of coastal marine hypoxia and benefitting fishery-based livelihoods in the Bay of Bengal. Shrimp farm production has decreased by an average of 30% in recent years owing to white spot disease.  Best management practices of this project are envisaged to increase the efficiency of production, thereby generating additional income for shrimp farmers.  The project will also endeavour to promote more efficient application of fertilisers and pesticides for rice cultivation, practiced by smallholder farmers and larger systems.  This has the potential to increase farmers production profits, thereby increasing per hectare income. This will in turn create employment opportunities for the population within the project area, thereby helping the realisation of the right to Decent Rural Employment. The project will strengthen the role of women and youth involvement in shrimp farm production and rice cultivation.  The project interventions are expected to directly benefit 10,000 local people, including 5000 females and 5000 males.  The project benefits directly complement the SDGs, Blue Economy including livelihoods and commitments under the UNFCCC and Paris Agreement.</narrative>
  </description>
